\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 AN ESSAY ON SPECULATIVE THOUGHT}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 IN THE ANCIENT NEAR EAST}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 H. and H. A. FRANKFORT \bullet  JOHN A.WILSON}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 THORKILD JACOBSEN . WILLIAM A. IRWIN}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 We should remember that even for us speculative thought is less}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 rigidly disciplined than any other form. Speculation\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 as the ety-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mology of the word shows\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 is an intuitive, an almost visionary,}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mode of apprehension. This does not mean, of course, that it is}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mere irresponsible meandering of the mind, which ignores reality}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 or seeks to escape from its problems. Speculative thought tran-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 scends experience, but only because it attempts to explain, to}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 unify, to order experience. It achieves this end by means of hy-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 potheses. If we use the word in its original sense, then we may say}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 that speculative thought attempts to underpin the chaos of experi-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ence so that it may reveal the features of a structure\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 order, co-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 herence, and meaning.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 underpin, but reveals only where you put the pins in, and you mistake the folds for structure}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In our own time speculative thought finds its scope more severe-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ly limited than it has been at any other period. For we possess in}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 science another instrument for the interpretation of experience,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 one that has achieved marvels and retains its full fascination. We}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 do not allow speculative thought, under any circumstances, to en-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 croach upon the sacred precincts of science. It must not trespass}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 on the realm of verifiable fact; and it must never pretend to a dig-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 nity higher than that of working hypotheses, even in the fields in}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 which it is permitted some scope.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The fundamental difference between the attitudes of modern}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and ancient man as regards the surrounding world is this: for mod-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ern, scientific man the phenomenal world is primarily an "It"; for}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ancient\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and also for primitive\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 man it is a "Thou."}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The correlation "subject-object'' is, of course, the basis of all}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 scientific thinking; it alone makes scientific knowledge possible.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The second mode of cognition is the curiously direct knowledge}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 which we gain when we "understand" a creature confronting us\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 its fear, let us say, or its anger. This, by the way, is a form of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 knowledge which we have the honor of sharing with the animals.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Now the knowledge which "I " has of "Thou" hovers between}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the active judgment and the passive "undergoing of an impres-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 sion"; between the intellectual and the emotional, the articulate}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and the inarticulate. "Thou" may be problematic, yet "Thou" is}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 somewhat transparent. "Thou" is a live presence, whose qualities}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and potentialities can be made somewhat articulate\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 not as a result}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 of active inquiry but because "Thou," as a presence, reveals itself.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 There is yet another important difference. An object, an "It,"}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 can always be scientifically related to other objects and appear as}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 part of a group or a series. In this manner science insists on seeing}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 "It"; hence, science is able to comprehend objects and events as}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ruled by universal laws which make their behavior under given}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 circumstances predictable. "Thou," on the other hand, is unique.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 "Thou" has the unprecedented, unparalleled, and unpredictable}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 character of an individual, a presence known only in so far as it re-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 veals itself. "Thou," moreover, is not merely contemplated or}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 understood but is experienced emotionally in a dynamic reciprocal}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 relationship. For these reasons there is justification for the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 aphorism of Crawley: "Primitive man has only one mode of}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 thought, one mode of expression, one part of speech\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the person-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 al." This does not mean (as is so often thought) that primitive}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 man, in order to explain natural phenomena, imparts human char-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 acteristics to an inanimate world. Primitive man simply does not}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 know an inanimate world. For this very reason he does not "per-sonify" inanimate phenomena nor does he fill an empty world}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 with the ghosts of the dead, as "animism" would have us believe.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The world appears to primitive man neither inanimate nor emp-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ty but redundant with life; and life has individuality, in man and}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 beast and plant, and in every phenomenon which confronts man\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the thunderclap, the sudden shadow, the eerie and unknown clear-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ing in the wood, the stone which suddenly hurts him when he stum-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 bles while on a hunting trip. Any phenomenon may at any time face}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 him, not as "It," but as "Thou." In this confrontation, "Thou"}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 reveals its individuality, its qualities, its will. "Thou" is not con-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 templated with intellectual detachment; it is experienced as life}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 confronting life, involving every faculty of man in a reciprocal re-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 lationship. Thoughts, no less than acts and feelings, are subordi-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 nated to this experience.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 All experience of "Thou" is highly individual; and early}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 man does, in fact, view happenings as individual events. An ac-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 count of such events and also their explanation can be conceived}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 only as action and necessarily take the form of a story. In other}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 words, the ancients told myths instead of presenting an analysis}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 or conclusions. We would explain, for instance, that certain at-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mospheric changes broke a drought and brought about rain. The}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Babylonians observed the same facts but experienced them as the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 intervention of the gigantic bird Imdugud which came to their res-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 cue. It covered the sky with the black storm clouds of its wings}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and devoured the Bull of Heaven, whose hot breath had scorched}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the crops.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 eg snake shedding skin.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In telling such a myth, the ancients did not intend to provide en-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tertainment. Neither did they seek, in a detached way and without}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ulterior motives, for intelligible explanations of the natural phe-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 nomena. They were recounting events in which they were involved}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 to the extent of their very existence. They experienced, directly, a}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 conflict of powers, one hostile to the harvest upon which they de-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 pended, the other frightening but beneficial: the thunderstorm re-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 prieved them in the nick of time by defeating and utterly destroy-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ing the drought. The images had already become traditional at the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 time when we meet them in art and literature, but originally they}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 must have been seen in the revelation which the experience en-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tailed. They are products of imagination, but they are not mere}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 fantasy. It is essential that true myth be distinguished from legend,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 saga, fable, and fairy tale. All these may retain elements o\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u163\'A3 the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 myth. And it may also happen that a baroque or frivolous imagina-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tion elaborates myths until they become mere stories. But true}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 myth presents its images and its imaginary actors, not with the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 playfulness of fantasy, but with a compelling authority. It per-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 petuates the revelation of a "Thou."}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 We have hitherto been at pains to show that for primitive man}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 thoughts are not autonomous, that they remain involved in the curi-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ous attitude toward the phenomenal world which we have called a}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 confrontation of life with life. Indeed, we shall find that our cate-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 gories of intellectual judgment often do not apply to the complexes}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 of cerebration and volition which constitute mythopoeic thought.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The basic distinction of modern thought is that between sub-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 jective and objective. On this distinction scientific thought has based}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 a critical and analytical procedure by which it progressively re-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 duces the individual phenomena to typical events subject to uni-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 versal laws. Thus it creates an increasingly wide gulf between our}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 perception of the phenomena and the conceptions by which we}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 make them comprehensible. We see the sun rise and set, but we}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 think of the earth as moving round the sun. We see colors, but we}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 describe them as wave-lengths. We dream of a dead relative, but}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 we think of that distinct vision as a product of our own subcon-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 scious minds. Even if we individually are unable to prove these}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 almost unbelievable scientific views to be true, we accept them,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 because we know that they can be proved to possess a greater}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 degree of objectivity than our sense-impressions. In the immediacy}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 of primitive experience, however, there is no room for such a criti-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 cal resolution of perceptions. Primitive man cannot withdraw from}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the presence of the phenomena because they reveal themselves to}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 him in the manner we have described. Hence the distinction be-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tween subjective and objective knowledge is meaningless to him.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Symbols are treated in the same way. The primitive uses sym-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 bols as much as we do; but he can no more conceive them as sig-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 nifying, yet separate from, the gods or powers than he can con-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 sider a relationship established in his mind\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 such as resemblance\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 as connecting, and yet separate from, the objects compared. Hence}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 there is coalescence of the symbol and what it signifies, as there is}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 coalescence of two objects compared so that one may stand for the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 other.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 An example of the coalescence of a symbol and the thing it stands}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 for is the treating of a person's name as an essential part of him\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 as if it were, in a way, identical with him. We have a number of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 pottery bowls which Egyptian kings of the Middle Kingdom had}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 inscribed with the names of hostile tribes in Palestine, Libya, and}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Nubia; the names of their rulers; and the names of certain rebel-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 lious Egyptians. These bowls were solemnly smashed at a}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ritual, possibly at the funeral of the king's predecessor; and the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 object of this ritual was explicitly stated. It was that all these ene-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mies, obviously out of the pharaoh's reach, should die. But if we}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 call the ritual act of the breaking of the bowls symbolical, we miss}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the point. The Egyptians felt that real harm was done to the ene-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mies by the destruction of their names. The occasion was even}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 used to cast a propitious spell of wider scope. After the names of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the hostile men, who were enumerated "that they should die,"}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 were added such phrases as: "all detrimental thought, all detri-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mental talk, all detrimental dreams, all detrimental plans, all detri-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mental strife," etc. Mentioning these things on the bowls to be}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 smashed diminished their actual power to hurt the king or lessen}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 his authority.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In the second place, we should note the concrete character at-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tributed to life in the statement that the gods withheld life in their}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 hands. In case one is inclined to see in this phrase a figure of speech,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 it is well to remember that Gilgamesh and, in another myth,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Adapa are given a chance to gain eternal life simply by eating life}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 as a substance. Gilgamesh is shown the "plant of life," but a ser-pent robs him of it. Adapa is offered bread and water of life when}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 he enters heaven, but he refuses it on the instruction of the wily}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 god Enki. In both cases the assimilation of a concrete substance}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 would have made the difference between death and immortality.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 objective. If science, as we have said before, re-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 duces the chaos of perceptions to an order in which typical events}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 take place according to universal laws, the instrument of this con-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 version from chaos to order is the postulate of causality. Primitive}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 thought naturally recognized the relationship of cause and effect,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 but it cannot recognize our view of an impersonal, mechanical, and}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 lawlike functioning of causality. For we have moved far from the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 world of immediate experience in our search for true causes, that}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 is, causes which will always produce the same effect under the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 same conditions. We must remember that Newton discovered the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 concept of gravitation and also its laws by taking into account three}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 groups of phenomena which are entirely unrelated to the merely}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 perceptive observer: freely falling objects, the movements of the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 planets, and the alternation of the tides. Now the primitive mind}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 cannot withdraw to that extent from perceptual reality. Moreover,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 it would not be satisfied by our ideas. It looks, not for the "how,"}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 but for the "who," when it looks for a cause.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He looks for a pur-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 poseful will committing an act. If the rivers refuse to rise, it is not}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 suggested that the lack of rainfall on distant mountains adequately}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 explains the calamity. When the river does not rise, it has refused}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 to rise. The river, or the gods, must be angry with the people who}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 depend on the inundation. At best the river or the gods intend to}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 convey something to the people. Some action, then, is called for.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 We know that, when the Tigris did not rise, Gudea the king went}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 to sleep in the temple in order to be instructed in a dream as to the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 meaning of the drought. In Egypt, where annual records of the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 heights of the Nile flood were kept from the earliest historical}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 times, the pharaoh nevertheless made gifts to the Nile every year}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 about the time when it was due to rise. To these sacrifices, which}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 An Egyptian story puts into the mouth of a}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Syrian prince this sweeping statement to an envoy who had come}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 to him from the land of the Nile: "For (the imperial god) Amon}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 founded all lands. He founded them, but first he founded the land}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ofEgypt^ from which thou hast come. For skilled work came forth}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 from it to reach this place where I am, and teaching came from it}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 to reach this place where I am."3}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Because the source is Egyptian,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 we cannot be sure that a prince of Syria actually did say such words,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 acknowledging Egyptian leadership in learning and craftsmanship,}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 but this story from Egypt carries the assurance that it was a com-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 forting doctrine to those who believed themselves to live at the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 center of the world.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Let us return again to the geography of Egypt. We have the pic-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ture of the green gash of life cutting through the brown stretches}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 of nonlife. Let us examine the mechanics of the Egyptian scene.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Nile cuts north out of Africa, surmounts five rocky cataracts,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and finally empties into the Mediterranean. These cataracts form}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the barriers of Egypt against the Hamitic and Negro peoples to}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the south just as effectively as the deserts and the sea bar Libyan}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and Semitic peoples to the north, east, and west. In the morning}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the sun rises in the east, it crosses the sky by day, and it sets in}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the west in the evening. Of course, you know that; but it is im-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 portant enough in Egypt to deserve repeated mention, because the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 daily birth, journey, and death of the sun were dominating fea-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tures of Egyptian life and thought. In a country essentially rain-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 less, the daily circuit of the sun is of blazing importance. We}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 might think that there was too much sun in Egypt, that shade was}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 a welcome necessity; but the Egyptian hated the darkness and the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 cold and stretched himself happily to greet the rising sun. He saw}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 that the sun was the source of his life. At night "the earth is in}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 darkness, as if it were dead."4}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 So the personification of the sun's}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 power, the sun-god, was the supreme god and the creator-god.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Then, just as life is at its lowest ebb, the Nile River stirs slug-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 gishly and shows a pulse of power. Through the summer it swells}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 slowly but with increasing momentum until it begins to race with}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mighty waters, burst its banks, and rush over the miles of flat land}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 lying on each side. Great stretches of moving, muddy water cover}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the land. In a year of a high Nile they encroach upon the little vil-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 lage islands standing up out of the fields, nibble at the mud-brick}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 houses, and bring some of them tumbling down. From inert, dusty}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 wastes, the land has turned to a great shallow stream, which car-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ries a refertilizing load of silt. Then the peak of the flood passes,}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and the waters become more sluggish. Out of the flooded stretches}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 there appear little peaks of soil, refreshed with new, fertile mud.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The-torpor of men disappears; they wade out into the thick mud}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and begin eagerly sowing their first crop of clover or grain. Life}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 has come again to Egypt. Soon a broad green carpet of growing}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 fields will complete the annual miracle of the conquest of life over}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 death.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 These, then, were the two central features of the Egyptian}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 scene: the triumphant daily rebirth of the sun and the triumphant}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 annual rebirth of the river. Out of these miracles the Egyptians}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 drew their assurance that Egypt was the center of the universe and j}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 their assurance that renewed life may always be victorious over}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 death.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It is necessary to make some qualification to a picture which}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 has been presented in terms of a free gift of life and fertility.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Egypt was rich but not prodigal: the fruit did not drop from the |}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 trees for indolent farmers. The sun and the Nile did combine to}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 bring forth renewed life, but only at the cost of a battle against}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 death. The sun warmed, but in the summer it also blasted. The Nile}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 brought fertilizing water and soil, but its annual inundation was an-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tic and unpredictable. An exceptionally high Nile destroyed canals,}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 dams, and the homes of men. An exceptionally low Nile brought}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 famine. The inundation came quickly and moved on quickly; con-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 stant, back-breaking work was necessary to catch, hold, and doleout the waters for the widest and longest use. The desert was al-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ways ready to nibble away at the cultivation and turn fertile silt}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 into arid sand. The desert in particular was a terrible place of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 venomous serpents, lions, and fabulous monsters. In the broad}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 muddy stretches of the Delta, jungle-like swamps had to be drained}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and cleared to make arable fields. For more than a third of every}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 year the hot desert winds, the blasting sun, and the low Nile}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 brought the land within sight of death, until the weather turned}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and the river brought abundant waters again. Thus Egypt was rich}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and blessed in contrast with her immediate neighbors, but within}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 her own territory she experienced struggle, privations, and dangers}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 which made the annual triumph real. There was a sense that the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 triumph was not an automatic privilege but that it must be earned}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 at some cost.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Navigation on the Nile employed the power of the current in}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 moving north. In moving south, boats raised the sail in order to}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 take advantage of the prevailing north wind, which would push}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 them against the current. Since this was normal, it became the ideal}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 for any world, including the afterlife. Into their tombs the Egyp-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tians put two model boats, which might be projected by magic into}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the next world for navigation there. One boat had the sail down,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 for sailing north with the current on the waters of the other world;}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Thy path is filled with boulders and pebbles, and there}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 is no passable track, for it is overgrown with reeds, thorns, bram-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 bles, and wolfs pad. The ravine is on one side of thee, while the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mountain rises on the other."7The interesting result of uniformity is the way in which it ac-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 centuates any exceptional bit of relief that happens to break the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 monotonous regularity. Out in the desert one is conscious of every}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 hillock, of every spoor of an animal, of every desert duststorm, of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 every bit of movement. The rare irregular is very striking in an}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 environment of universal regularity. It has animation; it has life}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 within the dominating pattern of nonlife. So also in Egypt the pre-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 vailing uniformity of landscape threw into high relief anything}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 which took exception to that uniformity. A solitary tree of some}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 size, a peculiarly shaped hill, or a storm-cut valley was so excep-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tional that it took on individuality. Man who lived close to nature}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 endowed the exceptional feature with animation; it became in-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 spirited to his mind.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Egyptians}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 might\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and did\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 personify almost anything: the head, the belly,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the tongue, perception, taste, truth, a tree, a mountain, the sea, a}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 city, darkness, and death. But few of these were personified with}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 regularity or with awe; that is, few of them reached the stature of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 gods or demigods. They were forces with which man had the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 "Thou" relation. And it is a little difficult to think of anything in}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the phenomenal world with which he might not have that relation}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 as indicated in scenes and texts. The answer is that he might have}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the "Thou" relation with anything in the phenomenal world.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It comes out in his literature, where the best}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 products show a deliberate and sonorous parallelism of members,}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 which achieve dignity and cadence, even though it seems monot-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 onous and repetitive to modern ears.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Let us illustrate this literary balance by quotations from a text}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 giving a statement of one of the Egyptian kings:}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Give heed to my utterances / hearken to them.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I speak to you / I make you aware}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 That I am the son of Re / who issued from his body.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I sit upon his throne in rejoicing / since he established me}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 as king / as lord of this land.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 My counsels are good / my plans come to pass.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I protect Egypt / I defend it.9}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The same balance comes out in the Egyptian's cosmology and}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 his theology, where he sought for a counterpoise to each observed}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 phenomenon or each supernatural element. If there is a sky above,}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 there must be a sky below; each god must have his goddess con-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 sort, even though she has no separate divine function but is simply a}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 feminine counterpart of himself. Some of this striving for bilateral}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 symmetry seems to us strained, and undoubtedly artificial concepts}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 did arise in the search to find a counterpoise for anything observed}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 or conceived. However, the psychological desire for balance}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 which drew forth the artificial concept was not itself artificial but}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 was a deeply engrained desire for symmetric poise.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In the dogma that arose in magnification of the rising sun the}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 grateful joy of all creation at the renewed appearance of the morn-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ing sun was expressed again and again. The contrast between eve-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ning and morning was a contrast between death and life. "When}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 thou settest on the western horizon, the land is in darkness in the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 manner of death ... . (but) when the day breaks, as thou risest on}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the horizon, ... . they awake and stand upon their feet, ... . they}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 live because thou hast arisen for them,"13}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Not only does mankind}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 join in this renewal of life, but "all beasts prance upon their feet,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and everything that flies or flutters,"14 and "apes worship him;}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 'Praise to thee!' (say) all beasts with one accord."15}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Egyptian}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 pictures show this morning worship of the sun by animals: the apes}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 stretching out limbs which had been cooled at night, in apparent}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 salutation to the warmth of the sun, or the ostriches limbering up}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 at dawn by dancing a stately pavan in the first rays of the sun.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Such observed phenomena were visible proofs of the communion of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 men, beasts, and the gods.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Egyptian conceived of the earth as a flat platter with a cor-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 rugated rim. The inside bottom of this platter was the flat alluvial}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 plain of Egypt, and the corrugated rim was the rim of mountain}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 countries which were the foreign lands. This platter floated in}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 water. There were the abysmal waters below, on which the platter}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 rested, called by the Egyptian "Nun." Nun was the waters of the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 underworld, and, according to one continuing concept, Nun was}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the primordial waters out of which life first issued. Life still issued}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 from these underworld waters, for the sun was reborn every day}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 out of Nun, and the Nile came pouring forth from caverns which}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 were fed from Nun. In addition to being the underworld waters,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Nuri was the waters encircling the world, the Okeanos which}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 formed the outermost boundary, also called the "Great Circuit" or}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the "Great Green." Thus it was clear that the sun, after its nightly}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 journey under the world, must be reborn beyond the eastern hori-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 zon out of those encircling waters, just as all the gods had original-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ly come forth out of Nun.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Above the earth was the inverted pan of the sky, setting the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 outer limit to the universe. As we have already said, the craving}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 for symmetry, as well as a sense that space is limited, called forth a}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 counterheaven under the earth, bounding the limits of the under-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 world. This was the universe within which man and the gods and}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the heavenly bodies operated.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Various qualifications to this picture are immediately necessary.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Our picture gives the vault of heaven as suspended by apparent}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 levitation above the earth. That would appeal to the ancient Egyp-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tian as dangerous, and he would ask for some visible means of sup-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 port. As we have already said, he provided various means of sup-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 port in various concepts, the incompatibility of which he cheerfully}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ignored. The simplest mechanism was four posts set on earth to}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 carry the weight of heaven. These were at the outer limits of the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 earth, as is indicated by such texts as: "I have set .. . . the terror}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 of thee as far as the four pillars of heaven,"16}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and the number four}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 suggests that they were placed at the four points of the compass.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Fortunately, this arrangement appealed to the Egyptian as being}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 both strong and permanent: "(As firm) as heaven resting upon its}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 four posts" is a simile used more than once.17}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 These undying stars they took as the symbol of the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 dead who triumphed over death and went on into eternal life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 That north section of heaven was in early times an important part}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 of the universe. Visibly there was no death there; therefore, it}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 must be the place of the eternal blessedness for which Egyptians}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 longed. In the early mortuary texts, which we moderns call the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pyramid Texts, the goal of the deceased was the region of Dat in}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the northern part of heaven, where he would join the circumpolar}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 stars "which know no destruction" and thus live forever himself.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 There were located their Elysian Fields, the "Field of Reeds" and}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the "Field of Offerings," in which the dead would live as an akh,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 an "effective" spirit.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 We have mentioned how broad sheets of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 water cover Egypt when the Nile inundation is at its height and}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 how the sinking of the waters brings into view the first isolated}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 peaks of mud, refreshed with new fertile silt. These would be the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 first islands of promise for new life in a new agricultural year.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 As}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 these first hillocks of slime lift their heads out of the floodwaters}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 into the baking warmth of the sun, it is easy to imagine that they}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 sputter and crackle with new life. The modern Egyptians believe}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 that there is special life-giving power in this slime, and they are}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 not alone in this belief. A little less than three centuries ago there}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 was a scientific controversy about spontaneous generation, the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ability of apparently inorganic matter to produce living organisms.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 One Englishman wrote that if his scholarly opponent doubted}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 that life came into being through putrefaction which went on in}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 mud or slime, "let him go to Egypt, and there he will find the fields}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 swarming with mice begot of the mud of Nylus, to the great calam-ity of the inhabitants.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It is not hard to believe that animal life may come out of this highly charged mud.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The creation texts which we have discussed earlier have been}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 more strictly in physical terms: the god separating earth from sky}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 or giving birth to air and moisture. This new text turns as far as}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the Egyptian could turn toward a creation in philosophical terms:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 the thought which came into the heart of a god and the command-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ing utterance which brought that thought into reality. This crea-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tion by thought conception and speech delivery has its experiential}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 background in human life: the authority of a ruler to create by}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 command. But only the use of physical terms such as "heart" for}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 thought and "tongue" for command relate the Memphite Theology}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 to the more earthy texts which we have been considering. Here, as}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Professor Breasted has pointed out, we come close to the back-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ground of the Logos doctrine of the New Testament: " In the begin-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 was God."}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Before undertaking this difficult text itself, we should lay out for}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ourselves the known factors that play into the interpretation of the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 text. First, the Memphite text takes off from the creation stories}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 which I have already recounted: Atum coming into being out of}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Nun, the primeval waters, and Atum bringing his Ennead of gods}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 into existence. The Memphite text is aware that these were pre-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 vailing concepts in Egypt. In place of discarding them as competi-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tive, it wishes to subsume them into a higher philosophy, to take}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 advantage of them by pointing out that they belong to a higher}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 system.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 They occur normally as a pair of related attributes of the sun-god:}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Hu, "authoritative utterance," that speech which is so effective}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 that it creates, and Sia, ''perception," the cognitive reception of a}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 situation, an object, or an idea. Hu and Sia were attributes that}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 carried governing authority. In the Pyramid Texts the ruling god}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 leaves his shrine and surrenders his office to the deceased king, be-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 cause the latter "has captured Hu, has control of Sia" (300). In}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 our Memphite text these two attributes of power are taken in}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 material terms: the heart is the organ which conceives thought,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 and the tongue is the organ which creates the conceived thought as}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 a phenomenal actuality. All this is credited to the activity of the}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Memphite god Ptah, who is himself thought and speech in every}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 heart and on every tongue, and thus was the first creative principle,}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 just as he remains now.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 "Thus it was discovered and understood that his (Ptah's)}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 power is greater than (that of the other) gods. And so Ptah rested}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 after he had made everything, as well as the divine order." Admit-}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 tedly the word "rested7}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ' introduces a parallel to the Genesis story}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 of God's resting on the seventh day. The translation "rested" is}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 defensible, but it is probably safer to render: "And so Ptah was}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 satisfied, after he had made everything."}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1}